{
  "image_name": "1a0c018e151e03094c2eda8a4b578b08.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how endometrial cancer staging changed from FIGO 2009 to FIGO 2023, using diagrams of the uterus and nearby pelvic structures to show where disease is located and how newer categories separate tumors by biologic behavior. Across stages I–IV, colored symbols distinguish aggressive versus non-aggressive histologic subtypes and the presence of substantial lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI).\n\nOverall, the update makes stage I more detailed by dividing tumors confined to the uterus according to depth of myometrial invasion, ovarian involvement in selected low-grade cases, and whether disease is limited to a polyp or endometrium. Stage II also becomes more nuanced, separating cervical stromal invasion from cases defined by substantial LVSI or aggressive histology with myometrial invasion.\n\nThe lower panels show spread beyond the uterus. Stage III covers local or regional extension, including spread to the uterine serosa, adnexa, vagina, parametrium, and pelvic or para-aortic lymph nodes, with visible distinctions between pelvic and para-aortic nodal disease and apparent micro- versus macrometastatic categories. Stage IV represents the furthest spread, with invasion of bladder or intestinal mucosa or distant metastases. In short, the figure emphasizes a shift from mainly anatomic staging to a combined anatomic and pathology-based system.",
